1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:01,900
Why does your refrigerator have to heat up

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:03,150
to keep your food cold?

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,420
It sounds backwards.

4
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:06,180
But put your hand on the side of a running

5
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,900
fridge, or near the grille at the bottom,

6
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:09,630
and you'll often feel warmth.

7
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:11,620
In a hot room, the sides of some models

8
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,770
get hotter than fifty degrees Celsius.

9
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:15,300
That's about a hundred and twenty

10
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,030
Fahrenheit.

11
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:17,690
That heat isn't a defect.

12
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,890
It's the whole trick.

13
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,080
And tucked away inside, right next to the

14
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:22,980
coldest part of the machine, there's a

15
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:23,910
second heater.

16
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:25,910
One that switches on on purpose.

17
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:28,320
Let's start with the way we talk about

18
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:28,930
cold.

19
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:31,330
We talk about cold as if it were a thing.

20
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:32,420
Let the cold out.

21
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:33,420
Keep the cold in.

22
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:34,870
But there's no such substance.

23
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:36,460
There is no cold to add.

24
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,460
What exists is heat: energy that flows

25
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,370
because of a difference in temperature.

26
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:42,750
And on its own, heat only flows one way.

27
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:43,990
From hotter to colder.

28
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:45,720
A hot mug warms your hands.

29
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:47,800
A cold drink never chills them by pushing

30
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:48,710
cold in.

31
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:50,860
Your hands are simply losing heat to it.

32
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:52,910
So a refrigerator doesn't make cold.

33
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:54,890
It removes heat.

34
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:56,740
Your milk is cold because heat has been

35
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:57,580
taken out of it.

36
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:58,850
Here's the problem.

37
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:00,740
Your kitchen is warmer than the inside of

38
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:01,490
your fridge.

39
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,700
Getting heat out of the cold box and into

40
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:05,540
the warm room means moving it in the one

41
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,590
direction it will never go by itself.

42
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,770
Physicists have a name for this rule.

43
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:11,660
It's the Clausius statement of the second

44
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:13,090
law of thermodynamics.

45
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,520
Moving heat from a cold place to a warmer

46
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:16,690
one takes work.

47
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,970
So your fridge works like a heat pump.

48
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:22,200
It spends electricity to push heat uphill,

49
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,170
out of your food and into your kitchen.

50
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:28,120
To pull that off, it uses one fluid

51
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:29,800
travelling around and around a sealed

52
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:30,370
loop.

53
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:31,520
The refrigerant.

54
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:33,560
Follow one drop of it all the way round,

55
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:35,190
and the whole machine makes sense.

56
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:37,290
The trick it relies on is boiling.

57
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:39,300
A liquid's boiling point depends on

58
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:39,950
pressure.

59
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:42,060
Lower the pressure, and it boils at a

60
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:43,070
lower temperature.

61
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:45,340
Raise the pressure, and the boiling point

62
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:45,990
climbs.

63
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:48,500
Household refrigerants boil far below

64
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:51,370
freezing, even at ordinary air pressure.

65
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:54,360
Isobutane, one of the common ones, boils

66
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:56,450
at about eleven degrees Fahrenheit.

67
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:57,630
Now, the loop.

68
00:01:57,860 --> 00:01:59,980
Stop one is the evaporator, a coil inside

69
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:00,690
the fridge.

70
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,400
Here the refrigerant is kept at low

71
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:04,600
pressure, so it boils, and boiling soaks

72
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:05,270
up heat.

73
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:07,350
That heat comes from inside the fridge.

74
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:09,440
From the air, the shelves, last night's

75
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,220
leftovers, and the warmth that keeps

76
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,290
creeping in through the walls and door.

77
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:15,090
This is where it all ends up.

78
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:16,830
Stop two is the compressor.

79
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:19,860
The cold, low pressure vapor flows into

80
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:22,040
it, and the compressor squeezes it into a

81
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:24,090
high pressure vapor that is much hotter.

82
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:25,700
Hot enough to be well above room

83
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:26,550
temperature.

84
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,540
This is where the electricity does its

85
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:29,410
main job.

86
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:31,330
The compressor is doing the work.

87
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,250
Stop three is the condenser.

88
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,740
Depending on the design, that's a coil on

89
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,920
the back, tubing hidden inside the side

90
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:40,380
walls, or a coil in a compartment at the

91
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:42,910
bottom with a fan blowing across it.

92
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,400
The vapor arriving here is hotter than

93
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:47,540
your kitchen, so heat flows out of it into

94
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,250
the room.

95
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:49,570
The easy direction.

96
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,100
As it loses heat, the vapor condenses back

97
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:54,350
into a liquid, still under high pressure.

98
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:56,570
Stop four is the expansion device.

99
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:58,560
In a typical household fridge it's a

100
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:00,940
capillary tube, a long, very thin tube

101
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:02,330
that restricts the flow.

102
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:04,420
It holds back the high pressure side from

103
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:05,790
the low pressure side.

104
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,160
Past it, the pressure drops, the boiling

105
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:10,340
point drops with it, and the refrigerant

106
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:11,790
starts to boil again.

107
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:14,820
In many fridges this tube is soldered to

108
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:16,900
the line carrying cold vapor back to the

109
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:19,200
compressor, so the warm liquid hands some

110
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:21,070
of its heat to that vapor on the way.

111
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:22,860
And then the refrigerant is back in the

112
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,210
evaporator, and the loop starts again.

113
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:26,830
Now do the accounting.

114
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:28,600
Energy is conserved.

115
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:30,480
So the heat the fridge dumps into your

116
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,800
kitchen equals all the heat it pulled out

117
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:35,400
of the inside, plus the electrical energy

118
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:36,970
it used to do it.

119
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:40,000
On a tough standard test, a household

120
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:43,000
compressor moves roughly one point four to

121
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:45,260
two units of heat for every unit of

122
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:46,850
electricity it uses.

123
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:49,920
So here's the missing context to the

124
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:50,570
title.

125
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:52,510
Heat only flows downhill.

126
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:54,260
For heat to leave the condenser on its

127
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,280
own, the refrigerant inside has to be

128
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:57,910
hotter than the air in your kitchen.

129
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:00,180
That's the compressor's whole job: push it

130
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:01,670
well above room temperature.

131
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:03,140
A condenser that stayed at room

132
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:04,970
temperature couldn't get rid of anything.

133
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:07,500
Dust, lint and pet hair on the condenser

134
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:09,520
block the airflow and make it run hotter,

135
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:11,880
so the compressor has to work harder and

136
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:12,510
longer.

137
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:14,600
Hotpoint says to clean the coils at least

138
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:15,650
once a year.

139
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:18,500
Some models don't need routine cleaning at

140
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:19,130
all.

141
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:20,590
Space matters too.

142
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:23,060
Manufacturers specify air gaps, and they

143
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:24,010
vary a lot.

144
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,560
One G E side by side asks for just an

145
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:27,950
eighth of an inch at the sides.

146
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:30,010
Samsung recommends at least five

147
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:31,860
centimetres, about two inches, at the

148
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:32,870
sides and back.

149
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:34,880
And putting a fridge next to an oven, a

150
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:37,300
dishwasher, a heating vent, or in direct

151
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:39,240
sunlight makes it harder for it to shed

152
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:39,870
heat.

153
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:41,440
Energy guides and installation

154
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:43,690
instructions say to avoid it.

155
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:45,750
Now for that second heater.

156
00:04:46,020 --> 00:04:47,930
The evaporator stays below freezing.

157
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:49,720
And the air inside your fridge carries

158
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:52,160
moisture, from your food, and from warm,

159
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:53,940
humid kitchen air that rushes in every

160
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,250
time you open the door.

161
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:57,340
That moisture condenses on the cold coil

162
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:58,170
and freezes.

163
00:04:58,960 --> 00:04:59,440
Frost.

164
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:01,090
Frost conducts heat very poorly.

165
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,840
A layer of it insulates the coil and

166
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:04,940
chokes the airflow, so the fridge cools

167
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,570
worse and wastes energy.

168
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:08,740
A frost free fridge solves it with an

169
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:11,360
electric heater, mounted on or just below

170
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:12,530
the evaporator.

171
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:15,400
Often a metal rod, or a heating element

172
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:17,010
inside a glass tube.

173
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:19,060
While it's on, it can draw about three

174
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:21,090
times as much power as the compressor.

175
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:23,580
In the two service manuals we checked, the

176
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,180
heater drew about four hundred and fifty

177
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:27,190
to four hundred and seventy watts.

178
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:29,024
The compressor, roughly a hundred and

179
00:05:29,044 --> 00:05:31,100
twenty to a hundred and sixty.

180
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:33,180
But the heater only runs for minutes at a

181
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:33,810
time.

182
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:35,450
So when does it switch on?

183
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:37,620
It goes by how long the compressor has

184
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:38,570
been running.

185
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:41,360
Older timers start a defrost about every

186
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:42,630
eight hours of running.

187
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,450
Newer controls adapt: keep the doors shut,

188
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,280
and some models wait a hundred hours or

189
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:47,850
more.

190
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:48,990
Then there's the water.

191
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:51,280
In many fridges, the melted frost drains

192
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:53,140
through a tube to a shallow pan down near

193
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:54,050
the condenser.

194
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:56,380
There, the condenser's warmth, and in many

195
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:58,740
models its fan, evaporate the water back

196
00:05:58,760 --> 00:05:59,520
into your kitchen.

197
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:02,460
The fridge uses its own waste heat to get

198
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:04,070
rid of its own meltwater.

199
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:05,930
And there's more deliberate heat.

200
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:08,600
Many fridges warm the edges of the cabinet

201
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:10,740
where the doors seal, either with a loop

202
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:13,260
of warm refrigerant tubing or with small

203
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:14,570
electric heaters.

204
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:16,940
The point is to stop condensation forming

205
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:18,030
on the outside.

206
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:20,320
Some models even have an energy saver

207
00:06:20,340 --> 00:06:22,590
switch that turns that heater off.

208
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:25,380
So the box that keeps your food cold has

209
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:27,940
warm edges around its doors, and a heater

210
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,590
mounted right next to its coldest coil.

211
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:33,000
All of that shows up on your electricity

212
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:33,770
bill.

213
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:36,190
The good news is how small it has become.

214
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:38,860
A new, efficient refrigerator uses a few

215
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:40,650
hundred kilowatt hours a year.

216
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:43,100
One eighteen cubic foot top freezer model

217
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:45,500
on the ENERGY STAR list is rated at three

218
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:47,480
hundred and sixty three kilowatt hours a

219
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:48,050
year.

220
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:50,160
That's about ten percent under the federal

221
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:51,510
limit for its size.

222
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:54,180
In nineteen seventy four, the average new

223
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:56,710
fridge used more than eighteen hundred.

224
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:59,480
New fridges use roughly a quarter of the

225
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,680
energy of mid seventies models, and they

226
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:02,550
hold more.

227
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:04,540
Which brings us to the old advice about

228
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:05,950
the open fridge door.

229
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:08,370
Leaving it open won't cool your kitchen.

230
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:10,320
Every bit of heat it pulls out goes

231
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:12,140
straight back into the same room through

232
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:14,360
the condenser, along with heat from the

233
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:15,790
electricity it uses.

234
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:17,770
So the room ends up warmer.

235
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,780
All you get is a brief puff of cold air at

236
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:21,490
the door.

237
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:23,960
So your fridge doesn't make cold.

238
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:25,140
It moves heat.

239
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:27,680
Out of the cold box, around a loop of

240
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:29,580
boiling and squeezing, and into your

241
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:31,840
kitchen, with a little extra added on the

242
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:32,430
way.

243
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:34,240
Much of that warmth on the side of the

244
00:07:34,260 --> 00:07:36,820
cabinet is heat that came from inside your

245
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:37,530
fridge.

246
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:40,140
Next time on The Hidden Mechanism, another

247
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,520
everyday machine that works in a way you'd

248
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:44,230
never guess from the outside.

